// 4szi.1 — process-local memo for the test-runner's toolchain fp.
// Before this memo, every cached_fixture_capture call fed
// read_or_compute_toolchain_fp, which always ran a `find -newer`
// shell-exec to validate the sidecar. The fp is invariant within
// any one bs2 invocation, so the memo holds for the whole process
// after the first compute.
//
// What this verifies — the WRITE-ONCE contract, NOT a marker round-trip.
// The memo (g_test_toolchain_fp_memo) is process-GLOBAL and shared with
// read_or_compute_toolchain_fp, which every fixture-using test warms via
// avra_test_toolchain_fp_set. Under the shared-process parallel runner
// (d4jv — test files run as @deferred_init units across worker threads in
// ONE process), a sibling fixture test sharded before this one publishes
// the real fp FIRST. A "set marker; get == marker" round-trip is therefore
// unobservable: write-once makes our set a no-op and get() returns the
// sibling's value (the failure that flaked the seed-train per shard order).
//
// So we assert the property that holds regardless of who wrote first:
// after any set the memo is published (non-empty), and a second, distinct
// set NEVER changes it. This is deterministic whether the memo was cold (we
// win the write) or already warmed (a sibling won) — once published the
// value is immutable, so there is no race between get() and the later set.

// t-n832 — this spec USED to publish its probe value into the memo whenever
// it ran first (a cold memo, i.e. whenever no sibling fixture had warmed it
// yet). That was not harmless: the memo is write-once and is an input to
// every fixture cache KEY, so the probe became the toolchain fingerprint for
// the whole process — a key that never changes when the compiler does. Every
// capture written under it replayed on later runs as though current, and a
// stale capture surfaced as a bare assertion FAIL with no source diff to
// explain it (quote_stmt_list_splice / comptime_fold / cli_metadata, red on
// rc-strict across runs but green in isolation every time).
//
// Two things now prevent that, and this spec still passes under both:
//   1. read_or_compute_toolchain_fp trusts the memo only when it is SHAPED
//      like sha256_hex output, so a probe can no longer key anything.
//   2. warm_toolchain_fp() below publishes the REAL fp first, so this spec
//      is never the first writer and its probe set is a proven no-op.

use @std.avrac.test_runner.{warm_toolchain_fp}

extern fn avra_test_toolchain_fp_get() -> string
extern fn avra_test_toolchain_fp_set(fp: string)

spec "4szi.1: toolchain fp memo is write-once (first writer wins)" {
    then "a set publishes a non-empty value" {
        // Warm with the REAL fp FIRST so this spec cannot be the memo's
        // first writer (t-n832). Our probe set is then a no-op whether the
        // memo was cold or a sibling had already warmed it, and the
        // published value is a genuine fingerprint either way.
        let real = warm_toolchain_fp()
        avra_test_toolchain_fp_set("4szi1-probe-cafebabe")
        real != "" && avra_test_toolchain_fp_get() == real
    }
    then "a second distinct set never changes the published value (write-once)" {
        // Capture whatever is published (ours, or a sibling's first write),
        // then prove a deliberately different set does not stick. Write-once
        // means the captured value is already frozen, so this holds with no
        // free-behind-the-reader hazard for concurrent getters.
        let published = avra_test_toolchain_fp_get()
        avra_test_toolchain_fp_set("4szi1-second-must-not-stick")
        published != "" && avra_test_toolchain_fp_get() == published
    }
}
